name: blobs
after: 22-bytes
intro: A file on a machine you were handed does not have to tell you what it
intro: is, and its name is whatever the last person felt like. Three programs
intro: for deciding that yourself: name it by what is in its first four bytes,
intro: say whether it is text or not, and pull the readable part out.
---
q: what.sh -- name a file by its first four bytes
spec: It takes one file and prints exactly one word, from this list, by what
spec: the file's first four bytes are:
spec:
spec:   7f 45 4c 46          elf
spec:   1f 8b (then any two) gzip
spec:   50 4b 03 04          zip
spec:   30 37 30 37          cpio
spec:   anything else        text
spec:
spec: With anything other than exactly one argument it exits 2.
file: what.sh
prog: #!/bin/sh
prog: [ $# -eq 1 ] || exit 2
prog: m=$(od -An -tx1 -N4 "$1" | tr -d ' \n')
prog: case "$m" in
prog: 7f454c46) echo elf ;;
prog: 1f8b*)    echo gzip ;;
prog: 504b0304) echo zip ;;
prog: 30373037) echo cpio ;;
prog: *)        echo text ;;
prog: esac
case: sh what.sh @bin@
case: sh what.sh archive/site-backup.tar.gz
case: sh what.sh archive.zip
case: sh what.sh archive.cpio
case: sh what.sh @txt@
case: sh what.sh; echo $?
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: the hard part is getting those four bytes into something you can compare -- after that it is one case
---
q: bytes.sh -- how much of a file is not text
spec: It takes one file and prints two lines and nothing else:
spec:
spec:   bytes N     how many bytes the file holds
spec:   binary N    how many of those are not printable ASCII
spec:
spec: A byte counts as printable when it is a tab, a newline, a carriage
spec: return, or falls in 32 to 126. Everything else is binary.
spec:
spec: With anything other than exactly one argument it exits 2.
file: bytes.sh
prog: #!/bin/sh
prog: [ $# -eq 1 ] || exit 2
prog: echo "bytes $(wc -c < "$1")"
prog: od -An -tu1 -v "$1" | awk '{ for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) if ($i < 9 || ($i > 13 && $i < 32) || $i > 126) n++ } END { print "binary", n + 0 }'
case: sh bytes.sh @bin@
case: sh bytes.sh @txt@
case: sh bytes.sh archive.zip
case: sh bytes.sh; echo $?
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: od can hand you every byte as a number, one screenful of columns at a time -- the counting is then arithmetic, not pattern matching
---
q: strings-in.sh -- the readable part of everything in a directory
spec: It takes a directory. For every regular file directly inside it, it
spec: prints the file's name -- the bare name, no path -- then one space,
spec: then the first run of 6 or more printable characters in that file.
spec:
spec: A file with no such run prints nothing at all. Subdirectories are
spec: skipped.
spec:
spec: With anything other than exactly one argument it exits 2.
file: strings-in.sh
prog: #!/bin/sh
prog: [ $# -eq 1 ] || exit 2
prog: for f in "$1"/*; do
prog: [ -f "$f" ] || continue
prog: s=$(strings -n 6 "$f" | head -n 1)
prog: [ -n "$s" ] && echo "${f##*/} $s"
prog: done
case: sh strings-in.sh samples
case: sh strings-in.sh src
case: sh strings-in.sh; echo $?
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: ${f##*/} strips every path component off a name, and a glob already hands you one file at a time
